🧠 Problem Goal

If any cell in the matrix is 0, then:

Set the entire row of that cell to 0

Set the entire column of that cell to 0

✅ Step-by-Step Approach
Step 1: Store matrix size
int m = matrix.length;
int n = matrix[0].length;


m = number of rows

n = number of columns

Step 2: Create marker arrays
boolean[] row = new boolean[m];
boolean[] col = new boolean[n];


These arrays are used to remember:

which rows contain at least one 0

which columns contain at least one 0

Initially:

All values are false (no row/column marked)

Step 3: Scan the matrix
for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        if (matrix[i][j] == 0) {
            row[i] = true;
            col[j] = true;
        }
    }
}


What happens here:

Traverse every cell in the matrix

If a 0 is found at position (i, j):

Mark row[i] = true → row i must be zeroed

Mark col[j] = true → column j must be zeroed

So now:

row[] contains all rows that need to be zero

col[] contains all columns that need to be zero

Step 4: Modify the matrix
for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        if (row[i] || col[j]) {
            matrix[i][j] = 0;
        }
    }
}


Now:

Again traverse every cell

If:

row i is marked OR

column j is marked

Then set matrix[i][j] = 0

This ensures:

All required rows are zero

All required columns are zero

⏱ Time Complexity

First scan: O(m × n)

Second scan: O(m × n)

Total: O(m × n)

💾 Space Complexity

row[] array → O(m)

col[] array → O(n)

Total extra space: O(m + n)

🧩 Why this works

You don’t modify the matrix while scanning, which prevents accidental propagation of zeros.
Instead, you:

First record positions

Then apply changes safely

🟢 Summary in simple terms

Remember which rows and columns contain 0

Loop again

Set those rows and columns to 0